    "findings": [
      "At zero extra margin, the real-photograph reconstruction has two prominent hooks along the ribbon. At 8px one remains prominent. At 16px both large hooks disappear; smaller photographic edge irregularity remains.",
      "The synthetic zero- and 8px-margin outputs visually recover the regular grid; editable conversion gives exact clean-mask agreement at 400px. The 16px-margin grid bends and shifts where useful observations have been removed.",
      "All six editable outputs retain geometry/paper checks and have no editor issues."
    ]
  },
  "conclusions": [
    "The ribbon annotation coverage changes the surviving central hooks, supporting input contamination as a cause. This does not prove the real hidden geometry or attribute every defect to annotation.",
    "A lower observed-region error as coverage decreases is not evidence of better reconstruction: in the synthetic control, the widest exclusion yields 0.00146% observed error but 2.70125% clean-mask error after editable export.",
    "The study adds no complete original-photo passes and no automatic occlusion detector. It does not change production masks, fitting, or acceptance gates."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Single real photograph and single known-grid control; no held-out occlusion accuracy.",
    "Only 42.90% to 54.32% of each mask remains scored because the prior coarse caps exclusion is retained.",
    "The widest uncertainty region excludes visible paper too. Real hidden colours and cutting paths are unknown.",
    "Independent geometric and editable rasterizers differ slightly; both results are retained.",
    "The real source has decorative flower caps beyond the regular grid, so full-original disagreement measures those excluded decorations too."
  ],
  "nextAction": "Use separate visible-material and complete-body supervision; avoid global uncertainty dilation. Independently test the observed browser/native resampling sensitivity with fixed target masks and configurations.",
